    VALUES
      (v_sec, $q${"blocks":[{"type":"text","content":"Work over the following decades would reveal the circuitry underlying center and surround mechanisms. Center responses are driven by excitatory bipolar cell input which is collected over a ganglion cell’s dendritic tree. Surround responses are driven predominantly by GABAergic amacrine cells that reside (laterally) further away and relay visual signals to the central ganglion cell. Center bipolar cells, and the bipolar cells that activate amacrine in the surround are driven by the same luminance change. Varying the luminance delivered to these two regions varies excitatory (bipolar) or inhibitory (amacrine) drive to the central ganglion cell and causes the firing patterns observed by Kuffler and others. For example, in an ON ganglion cell, a bright spot placed in the surround activates amacrine inhibition and antagonizes the excitatory effects of a center spot that drives bipolar cells. A bright spot placed in the surround of this neuron evokes only amacrine inhibition and so does not drive spikes. A dark spot placed in the surround inhibits the amacrine cell and reduces its inhibition of the ganglion cell, this disinhibition often leads ganglion cells to spike."}]}$q$::jsonb, $q$Work over the following decades would reveal the circuitry underlying center and surround mechanisms. Center responses are driven by excitatory bipolar cell input which is collected over a ganglion ce$q$, v_pos + 1, false,
