      (v_sec, $q${"blocks":[{"type":"text","content":"RW Rodieck modelled excitatory (center) and inhibitory (surround) portions of the receptive field with two Gaussians which offered great power in predicting ganglion cell responses to light<sup data-sup='59'>59,</sup><sup data-sup='60'>60</sup>. It also showed that a given ganglion cell encodes an edge (or small spot), and not a homogenous patch of visual space. Rodieck’s models also made predictions about how center-surround organization could be used by downstream brain regions. For example, if a neuron in such a region has access to multiple center-surround receptive fields from several neighbouring ganglion cells, the precise location of a visual stimulus or pattern could be easily decoded. The most common cell types in the human retina, ON and OFF midget and ON and OFF parasol cells (which account for ~80% of ganglion cells), all show center-surround receptive fields<sup data-sup='61'>61</sup>."}]}$q$::jsonb, $q$RW Rodieck modelled excitatory (center) and inhibitory (surround) portions of the receptive field with two Gaussians which offered great power in predicting ganglion cell responses to light59,60. It a$q$, v_pos + 1, false,
