       SET content = $q${"blocks":[{"type":"text","content":"<strong>Direction-selectivity:</strong> In the human and non-human primate visual system, it was generally thought that direction selective responses first arise in primary visual cortex. However, recent research has described direction selectivity in the primate retina<sup data-sup='70'>70,</sup><sup data-sup='71'>71</sup>. This puts the primate retina in line with several animal models commonly studied, such as rabbits and mice (as well as flies and fish), where direction selectivity (DS) arises in retinal ganglion cells<sup data-sup='72'>72</sup>, and where the circuitry underlying the DS computation is among the most studied in neuroscience. Early ideas came out of studies in insects and proposed that visual inputs from two spatially-offset locations were relayed to a single downstream neuron with differing delays<sup data-sup='73'>73</sup>. A moving object that activated the long-delay input before the short delay input drove neurons better since the delay allowed excitation from both locations to coincide. Motion in the opposite direct led to little coincidence and so the neuron could respond selectively to motion direction. Recent work in infects confirm aspects of this model, but retinal direction selectivity in mammals arises in ganglion cells through direction-selective GABAergic inhibition from starburst amacrine cells<sup data-sup='72'>72</sup>. These amacrine cells, named by Ted Famiglietti due to their unique ‘firework’ anatomical appearance, grow dendrites radially outwards from their cell bodies<sup data-sup='74'>74</sup>. Starburst amacrine cells are preferentially activated by visual stimuli moving centripetally along their dendritic tree (i.e. moving away from the soma)<sup data-sup='75'>75</sup>. The distal tip of each starburst dendrite connects with a ganglion cell. This ganglion cell in turn has the opposite preferred direction to its presynaptic starburst partner, since the amacrine cell releases GABA when activated<sup data-sup='76'>76</sup>. In a remarkable feat of wiring, ganglion cells preferring a particular direction of motion receive inhibition exclusively from starburst amacrine cells dendrites that point the opposite direction<sup data-sup='77'>77</sup>. As such, stimuli moving in a ganglion cell’s preferred direction drive very little inhibition, whereas stimuli moving in the ganglion cell’s null direction drive strong GABAergic inhibition, which vetoes the relatively symmetrical excitatory drive from bipolar cells during both preferred and null direction motion."},{"type":"image","src":"GABAergic","caption":"For a given starburst amacrine cell, its distal processes are predominantly activated by visual stimuli moving away from the soma. In turn, it’s processes in different quadrants specifically connect to nearby direction selective ganglion cells with a preferred direction opposite to preferred direction of the starburst processes that connect to it. Adapted from Briggman et al., Nature, 2011.","closed":true}]}$q$::jsonb,
