eleases GABA when activated<sup data-sup='76'>76</sup>. In a remarkable feat of wiring, ganglion cells preferring a particular direction of motion receive inhibition exclusively from starburst amacrine cells dendrites that point the opposite direction<sup data-sup='77'>77</sup>. As such, stimuli moving in a ganglion cell’s preferred direction drive very little inhibition, whereas stimuli moving in the ganglion cell’s null direction drive strong GABAergic inhibition, which vetoes the relatively symmetrical excitatory drive from bipolar cells during both preferred and null direction motion."},{"type":"image","src":"GABAergic","caption":"For a given starburst amacrine cell, its distal processes are predominantly activated by visual stimuli moving away from the soma. In turn, it’s processes in different quadrants specifically connect to nearby direction selective ganglion cells with a preferred direction opposite to preferred direction of the starburst processes that connect to it. Adapted from Briggman et al., Nature, 2011.","closed":true}]}$q$::jsonb, $q$Direction-selectivity: In the human and non-human primate visual system, it was generally thought that direction selective responses first arise in primary visual cortex. However, recent research has $q$, v_pos + 1, true,
